[2026-09-13 14:17:36,336][src.models.HILBERTWorker][INFO] - <informal_proof>
**Goal.**  
For a function \(f:\mathbb Z\to\mathbb Z\) prove  

\[
\bigl(\forall a,b\in\mathbb Z,\;f(2a)+2f(b)=f\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr)\bigr)
\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
\Bigl(\forall z,\;f(z)=0\Bigr)\;\lor\;
\Bigl(\exists c\in\mathbb Z,\;\forall z,\;f(z)=2z+c\Bigr).
\]

We work with the hypothesis  

\[
\tag{H} \forall a,b\in\mathbb Z,\;f(2a)+2f(b)=f\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Introducing the auxiliary function \(g\).

1. Define  

   \[
   g(z):=f(z)-2z\qquad(z\in\mathbb Z).
   \]

   Then \(f(z)=2z+g(z)\) for every integer \(z\).

2. Substitute the expression \(f=2\cdot+\;g\) into (H).  
   For any \(a,b\),

   \[
   \begin{aligned}
   f(2a)&=2\cdot(2a)+g(2a)=4a+g(2a),\\
   f(b)&=2b+g(b),\\
   f\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr)
   &=2\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr)+g\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr)\\
   &=2\bigl(2(a+b)+g(a+b)\bigr)+g\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr)\\
   &=4(a+b)+2g(a+b)+g\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr).
   \end{aligned}
   \]

   Hence (H) becomes  

   \[
   4a+g(2a)+4b+2g(b)=4(a+b)+2g(a+b)+g\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr).
   \]

3. Cancel the common term \(4(a+b)\) and rearrange:

   \[
   \boxed{\;g(2a)+2g(b)=2g(a+b)+g\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr)\;}\tag{1}
   \]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  First consequences of (1).

4. Put \(b=0\) in (1). Using the notation \(g_0:=g(0)\),

   \[
   g(2a)+2g_0=2g(a)+g\bigl(f(a)\bigr).\tag{2}
   \]

5. Put \(a=0\) in (1). We obtain  

   \[
   g_0+2g(b)=2g(b)+g\bigl(f(b)\bigr),
   \]
   whence  

   \[
   \boxed{\;g\bigl(f(b)\bigr)=g_0\quad\text{for every }b\in\mathbb Z\;}\tag{3}
   \]

   i.e. the value of \(g\) is constant on the image of \(f\).

6. Substitute (3) into (2). Because \(g\bigl(f(a)\bigr)=g_0\),

   \[
   g(2a)+2g_0=2g(a)+g_0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
   \boxed{\;g(2a)=2g(a)-g_0\;}\tag{4}
   \]

   for all integers \(a\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Passing to a truly additive function.

7. Define  

   \[
   h(z):=g(z)-g_0\qquad(z\in\mathbb Z).
   \]

   Then \(h(0)=0\) and \(g(z)=h(z)+g_0\).  
   Equation (4) becomes  

   \[
   h(2a)=2h(a)\qquad\text{for all }a.\tag{5}
   \]

8. Rewrite (1) with \(g=h+g_0\). Using (3) (which says \(h\bigl(f(\cdot)\bigr)=0\)) we get

   \[
   \begin{aligned}
   &(h(2a)+g_0)+2\bigl(h(b)+g_0\bigr)
