4. [DEFINITION] Finite case analysis tactic (`fin_cases`)
Module: Mathlib.Tactic.FinCases
Name: Lean.Elab.Tactic.finCases
Description: The `fin_cases` tactic performs case analysis on a hypothesis `h` of the form `h : A` where `A` is a finite type (i.e., `[Fintype A]` is available), or `h : a ∈ A` where `A` is a finite collection (either a `Finset`, `Multiset`, or `List`). 

For example, given a hypothesis `h : p` where `p : Fin 3`, applying `fin_cases h` will split the goal into three cases corresponding to the possible values of `p` (namely, `0`, `1`, and `2`).
Signature:  : Lean.ParserDescr✝

5. [DEFINITION] Case splitting for finite collections and fintypes
Module: Mathlib.Tactic.FinCases
Name: Lean.Elab.Tactic.finCasesAt
Description: The `finCasesAt` function implements the core of the `fin_cases` tactic. Given a goal `g` and a hypothesis `hyp` of the form `x ∈ A` where `A` is a finite collection (list, finset, or multiset), or of the form `x : A` where `A` is a fintype, it splits the goal into multiple subgoals - one for each possible value in the finite collection or fintype. For hypotheses of the form `x ∈ A`, it directly unfolds the cases. For `x : A` with `[Fintype A]`, it first converts the hypothesis to membership in the enumeration of `A`'s elements before case splitting.
Signature:  (g : MVarId) (hyp : FVarId) : MetaM (List MVarId)

6. [DEFINITION] Existential introduction refinement for metavariables
Module: Mathlib.Lean.Meta
Name: Lean.MVarId.existsi
Description: Given a metavariable `mvar` representing a proof goal and a list of expressions `es`, this function sequentially refines the goal using existential introduction (`∃`), assigning each expression in `es` to the corresponding witness in the existential statement. 

The process works by repeatedly:
1. Splitting an existential goal `∃ x, P(x)` into two subgoals: one for the witness and one for the remaining existential
2. Assigning the current expression from `es` to the witness subgoal
3. Continuing with the remaining existential subgoal

The function maintains the metavariable representing the current proof goal throughout this process.
Signature:  (mvar : MVarId) (es : List Expr) : MetaM MVarId
