<justification>
The functional equation  

\[
\forall a,b\in\mathbb Z,\qquad f(2a)+2f(b)=f\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr)
\]

forces the function \(f\) to be either the zero function or an affine function of the form \(f(z)=2z+c\) with a constant integer \(c\).

* From the equation with \(a=0\) we obtain  
  \(f\bigl(f(b)\bigr)=2f(b)+f(0)\).  
  With \(b=0\) we get  
  \(f\bigl(f(a)\bigr)=f(2a)+2f(0)\).  

  Equating the two expressions for \(f(f(x))\) yields  

  \[
  f(2x)=2f(x)-f(0)\qquad(\ast)
  \]

* Define \(g(x)=f(x)-f(0)\).  Then \((\ast)\) becomes \(g(2x)=2g(x)\).  

* Substituting \(f(x)=g(x)+f(0)\) back into \(f(f(x))=2f(x)+f(0)\) gives  

  \[
  g\bigl(g(x)+f(0)\bigr)=2g(x)+2f(0).
  \]

  The only integer‑valued solutions of these two relations are  

  * \(g\equiv 0\) (hence \(f\equiv 0\)), or  
  * \(g(x)=2x\) for all \(x\) (hence \(f(x)=2x+f(0)\)).

Both families indeed satisfy the original equation, as can be checked directly:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{(i)}\;f\equiv0 &: \;0+0=0,\\
\text{(ii)}\;f(x)=2x+c &: \; (4a+c)+(4b+2c)=4a+4b+3c
      =f\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr).
\end{aligned}
\]

Consequently the conclusion  

\[
\forall z,\; f(z)=0\;\vee\;\exists c,\;\forall z,\; f(z)=2z+c
\]

holds: either the first disjunct is true for every \(z\) (the zero function), or there exists a single constant \(c\) such that the second disjunct holds for all \(z\) (the affine function). No other integer‑valued functions satisfy the hypothesis, so the theorem is mathematically correct.
</justification>
