1. [THEOREM] Downward Integer Induction Principle
Module: Mathlib.Data.Int.Defs
Name: Int.le_induction_down
Description: Let $P$ be a property on integers and let $m$ be an integer. If:
1. The base case $P(m)$ holds, and
2. For any integer $n \leq m$, if $P(n)$ holds then $P(n-1)$ holds,

then for any integer $n \leq m$, the property $P(n)$ holds.
Signature:  {P : ℤ → Prop} {m : ℤ} (h0 : P m) (h1 : ∀ n : ℤ, n ≤ m → P n → P (n - 1)) (n : ℤ) : n ≤ m → P n

2. [THEOREM] Upward Integer Induction Principle
Module: Mathlib.Data.Int.Defs
Name: Int.le_induction
Description: Let $P$ be a property on integers, and let $m$ be an integer. If:
1. The base case $P(m)$ holds, and
2. For any integer $n \geq m$, if $P(n)$ holds then $P(n + 1)$ holds,

then for any integer $n \geq m$, the property $P(n)$ holds.
Signature:  {P : ℤ → Prop} {m : ℤ} (h0 : P m) (h1 : ∀ n : ℤ, m ≤ n → P n → P (n + 1)) (n : ℤ) : m ≤ n → P n

3. [THEOREM] Integer Induction Principle
Module: Mathlib.Data.Int.Defs
Name: Int.induction_on
Description: Let $p$ be a property on integers. To prove that $p(i)$ holds for all integers $i$, it suffices to:
1. Show that $p(0)$ holds (base case),
2. For any natural number $i$, if $p(i)$ holds then $p(i+1)$ holds (positive induction step), and
3. For any natural number $i$, if $p(-i)$ holds then $p(-i-1)$ holds (negative induction step).
Signature:  {p : ℤ → Prop} (i : ℤ) (hz : p 0) (hp : ∀ i : ℕ, p i → p (i + 1)) (hn : ∀ i : ℕ, p (-i) → p (-i - 1)) : p i
