Results for query: by_cases tactic
1. [DEFINITION] Pattern-matching case analysis tactic
Module: Mathlib.Tactic.CasesM
Name: Mathlib.Tactic.casesM
Description: The `casesm` tactic performs case analysis (`cases`) on hypotheses whose types match given patterns. It has several variants:
1. `casesm p` applies `cases` to any hypothesis `h : type` where `type` matches pattern `p`
2. `casesm p₁, ..., pₙ` applies `cases` to hypotheses matching any of the patterns
3. `casesm* p` efficiently repeats `casesm p` until no more matches are found
4. `casesm! p` only applies `cases` when it produces at most one subgoal

Example usage: `casesm* _ ∨ _, _ ∧ _` destructs all conjunctions and disjunctions in the current context.
Signature:  : Lean.ParserDescr✝

2. [DEFINITION] Strict pattern-matching case analysis tactic
Module: Mathlib.Tactic.CasesM
Name: Mathlib.Tactic.casesm!
Description: The tactic `casesm!` performs case analysis (`cases`) on hypotheses whose types match given patterns, but only when the case analysis produces at most one subgoal. It is a variant of the `casesm` tactic with stricter conditions on applicability.
Signature:  : Lean.ParserDescr✝

3. [DEFINITION] Pattern-matching case analysis implementation
Module: Mathlib.Tactic.CasesM
Name: Mathlib.Tactic.elabCasesM
Description: The function implements the core functionality for the `casesm` tactic, which performs case analysis on hypotheses matching given patterns. It takes an array of pattern terms and two boolean flags (`recursive` and `allowSplit`), then applies case analysis to all matching hypotheses in the current goal context. The `recursive` flag determines whether to recursively apply case analysis on newly generated hypotheses, while `allowSplit` controls whether to split the goal when multiple cases are produced.
Signature:  (pats : Array Term) (recursive allowSplit : Bool) : TacticM Unit

4. [DEFINITION] Backward-compatible case analysis tactic (`cases'`)
Module: Mathlib.Tactic.Cases
Name: Mathlib.Tactic.cases'
Description: The `cases'` tactic is a backward-compatible version of Lean 4's `cases` tactic, with a different syntax for naming pattern-matched variables. It performs case analysis on a hypothesis, generating subgoals for each possible constructor of the hypothesis's type. The syntax allows specifying variable names directly after `with`, unlike Lean 4's `cases` which uses a different naming convention.
Signature:  : Lean.ParserDescr✝

5. [DEFINITION] Cases tactic for inductive types
Module: Mathlib.Tactic.CasesM
Name: Mathlib.Tactic.casesType
Description: The tactic `cases_type` applies the `cases` tactic to a hypothesis `h` of the form `(I ...)` where `I` is one of the given inductive types. Multiple types can be specified, in which case the tactic will apply to any hypothesis matching any of them. The variant `cases_type*` repeats this process until no more applicable hypotheses are found, and `cases_type!` only applies the tactic if it results in at most one subgoal.

Example usage:
```lean
cases_type* Or And
```
This will destruct all conjunctions and disjunctions in the current goal.
Signature:  : Lean.ParserDescr✝

