[2026-09-13 14:54:29,241][src.models.HILBERTWorker][INFO] - <informal_proof>
We are given a function  

\[
f:\mathbb Z\longrightarrow\mathbb Z ,\qquad 
\forall a,b\in\mathbb Z\;,\;f(2a)+2f(b)=f\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr) \tag{E}
\]

and we must show  

\[
\forall z\in\mathbb Z,\; f(z)=0\ \lor\ \exists c\in\mathbb Z,\;\forall z,\;f(z)=2z+c .
\]

The proof proceeds by extracting from (E) a linear‑type description of \(f\) and then
determining the only possible slopes.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
**1.   First consequences of (E).**  

1.1.  Put \(a=0\) in (E).  Using the theorem *two_mul* (\(2\cdot n=n+n\)) we obtain  

\[
f(0)+2f(b)=f\bigl(f(b)\bigr)\qquad\forall b\in\mathbb Z. \tag{1}
\]

Hence  

\[
f\bigl(f(b)\bigr)=2f(b)+f(0). \tag{1'}
\]

1.2.  Put \(b=0\) in (E).  We get  

\[
f(2a)+2f(0)=f\bigl(f(a)\bigr)\qquad\forall a\in\mathbb Z. \tag{2}
\]

Together with (1') (applied to \(a\)) this yields  

\[
f(2a)+2f(0)=2f(a)+f(0)\qquad\forall a\in\mathbb Z,
\]

and therefore  

\[
\boxed{\,f(2a)=2f(a)-f(0)\,}\qquad\forall a\in\mathbb Z. \tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
**2.   An additive relation for \(f\).**  

2.1.  Replace the left‑hand side of (E) by the expression given by (3) and
replace the right‑hand side by (1').  For arbitrary \(a,b\),

\[
\bigl(2f(a)-f(0)\bigr)+2f(b)=2f(a+b)+f(0).
\]

2.2.  Rearranging and dividing by \(2\) (division by an integer is legitimate in
\(\mathbb Z\)) we obtain  

\[
f(a)+f(b)-f(a+b)=f(0)\qquad\forall a,b\in\mathbb Z. \tag{4}
\]

Equivalently,

\[
\boxed{\,f(a+b)=f(a)+f(b)-f(0)\,}. \tag{4'}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
**3.   Passing to a genuine additive map.**  

Define  

\[
g:\mathbb Z\to\mathbb Z,\qquad g(z):=f(z)-f(0).
\]

3.1.  Using (4') we compute  

\[
g(a+b)=f(a+b)-f(0)=\bigl(f(a)+f(b)-f(0)\bigr)-f(0)=g(a)+g(b).
\]

Thus  

\[
\boxed{\,g(a+b)=g(a)+g(b)\,}\qquad\forall a,b\in\mathbb Z. \tag{5}
\]

3.2.  The group \((\mathbb Z,+)\) is cyclic, generated by \(1\).  
From (5) we have for every \(n\in\mathbb Z\),

\[
g(n)=ng(1) \qquad\text{(prove by induction on \(|n|\)).}
\]

Hence there exists an integer \(k:=g(1)\) such that  

\[
\boxed{\,g(z)=kz\; \text{for all }z\in\mathbb Z. \,} \tag{6}
\]

3.3.  Returning to \(f\),

\[
f(z)=g(z)+f(0)=kz+f(0)\qquad\forall z\in\mathbb Z. \tag{7}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
**4.   Determining the possible values of \(k\).**  

Insert the expression (7) into the original functional equation (E).

4.1.  Compute the left‑hand side:

\[
\begin{aligned}
f(2a)+2f(b)
&= \bigl(k\cdot 2a+f(0)\bigr)+2\bigl(kb+f(0)\bigr) \\
&= 2ka+f(0)+2kb+2f(0) \\
&= 2k(a+b)+3f(0).
\end{aligned}
\]

(The step \(2a = a+a\) may be justified by *two_mul*.)

4.2.  Compute the right‑hand side:

\[
\begin{aligned}
f\bigl(f(a+b)\bigr)
&= f\bigl(k(a+b)+f(0)\bigr) \\
&= k\bigl(k(a+b)+f(0)\bigr)+f(0) \\
&= k^{2}(a+b)+k f(0)+f(0).
\end{aligned}
\]

4.3.  Equality of the two sides for every \(a,b\) forces equality of the
coefficients of the independent variable \((a+b)\) and of the constant term:

\[
\begin{cases}
2k = k^{2},\\[2mm]
3f(0)=k f(0)+f(0).
\end{cases}
\tag{8}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
**5.   Solving the system (8).**  

5.1.  From the first equation \(2k=k^{2}\) we obtain  

\[
k(k-2)=0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad k=0\ \text{or}\ k=2. \tag{9}
\]

5.2.  From the second equation  

\[
3f(0)=(k+1)f(0)\ \Longrightarrow\ (2-k)f(0)=0. \tag{10}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
**6.   Case analysis.**  

*Case 1:* \(k=0\).  
Then (10) becomes \(2f(0)=0\).  Using the theorem *mul_eq_zero*,
\(2\neq 0\) in \(\mathbb Z\); hence \(f(0)=0\).  
From (7) we obtain \(f(z)=0\) for every integer \(z\).

*Case 2:* \(k=2\).  
Equation (10) is automatically satisfied (the factor \(2-k\) vanishes), so
\(f(0)\) may be any integer.  Set \(c:=f(0)\).  Then (7) reads  

\[
f(z)=2z+c\qquad\forall z\in\mathbb Z .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
**7.   Conclusion.**  

For every integer \(z\) the function \(f\) is either identically zero, or there
exists a constant \(c\in\mathbb Z\) (namely \(c=f(0)\)) such that  

\[
f(z)=2z+c\quad\text{for all }z\in\mathbb Z .
\]

Thus  

\[
\forall z,\; f(z)=0\ \lor\ \exists c,\;\forall z,\;f(z)=2z+c,
\]

which is exactly the required statement. ∎
</informal_proof>
